 \begin{abstract}
-\noindent Algorithmic traders read prices and, by trading, move them. We ask what a price record reveals about the population of such traders, and for which decisions that is enough. Prices see the population only through its aggregate response to past prices, the feedback kernel: populations with the same kernel produce the same prices, profits and equilibria. Three consequences follow. Composition is \emph{invisible}: telling apart $K$ trader types whose rules lie within $\Delta$ of one another takes data of order $\Delta^{-(4K-2)}$, for any smooth family of rules, whether the observer waits or trades. Composition is \emph{irrelevant} for forecasting: knowing every group's position improves price forecasts by no more than the error of estimating the kernel, however close the groups are. Composition is \emph{needed} only for actions aimed at particular traders and once position limits bind; tagging all but one class of traders supplies it, and the last tag is worth nothing. Core results are machine-checked in Lean~4.
+\noindent An exchange can reduce feedback trading across a market or restrict a particular class of traders. We study what the public record reveals about the effects of these actions. For traders who follow a common public signal, the aggregate feedback response determines public outcomes under fixed causal strategies. A uniform reduction depends on this response; a class-specific restriction can meet a prudential ceiling in one population and fail in another with nearly indistinguishable public records. For a smooth one-parameter family, common initialization and Gaussian innovations, we construct $K$-type populations within radius $\Delta$ whose distinction requires at least order $\Delta^{-(4K-2)}$ observations under a uniform derivative-moment bound. For separated linear-memory classes, a full-rank derivative condition yields matching recovery from noisy deterministic responses. Class-tagged flows remove the spacing penalty for recovering weights once at most one memory class remains untagged. A Gaussian identity relates pairwise detection to forecast mismatch. A Bayesian predictor has a uniform cumulative log-loss bound under fixed stability and memory margins. We distinguish these theoretical results from the pending empirical tests and from the portions checked in Lean~4.
+
 \end{abstract}
 
