 \section{Introduction}\label{n:intro}
 
-Trend-following funds, execution algorithms and AI trading agents read recent prices, decide what to trade and, by trading, move the prices they read. Regulators are now deciding how far to identify them. China's securities regulator issued rules on program trading in 2024, and since July 2025 its exchanges classify accounts as high-frequency by their order rates.\footnote{Shanghai, Shenzhen and Beijing stock exchanges, implementing rules of 3 April 2025 for the CSRC Provisions on Program Trading of May 2024.} India requires every algorithmic order to carry an exchange-issued identifier.\footnote{SEBI circular SEBI/HO/MIRSD/MIRSD-PoD/P/CIR/2025/0000013, 4 February 2025.} Japan has registered high-speed traders since 2018, and its regulator studies their orders by trader and strategy.\footnote{Financial Services Agency, FSA Analytical Notes, 21 November 2024.} The EU requires firms to identify their algorithms in order records under MiFID~II. In the United States, the SEC withdrew its proposal on firms' use of predictive data analytics in June 2025. Behind these choices is a question this paper answers: what does a market's price record reveal about its population of feedback traders, and for which decisions is that enough?
-
-\paragraph{Three findings and one question.} Three findings from different literatures point to the same question. First, models of markets with fundamentalist and chartist traders \citep{brock1998,hommes2006} are fitted to prices to estimate the traders' shares, and the estimates are fragile: a recent survey reports nearly collinear parameters, switching parameters that are not significant, and estimates that depend on where the optimizer starts \citep{lux2018}. Second, large traders' positions in U.S. futures markets respond to past price changes, non-commercial traders following trends, yet give very little evidence of leading returns \citep{sanders2009}. Third, regulators who want to act on particular algorithms do not try to infer them from prices; they require identifiers. Each finding asks what the price record reveals about who trades, and what knowing who trades is good for.
-
-\paragraph{The answer.} Prices see a population of feedback traders only through one object, the market's \emph{feedback kernel}: the response, $m$ periods later, of total feedback flow to a unit move in the price signal. Populations with the same kernel produce the same prices, order flows, profits and equilibria (Theorem~\ref{n:suff}). This single fact has three sharp consequences, and together they say where knowledge of the population is worth having.
-\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
-\item \emph{Composition is invisible.} Telling apart $K$ types of trader whose rules lie within $\Delta$ of one another takes data of order $\Delta^{-(4K-2)}$. This holds for any smooth family of trading rules, not only exponential memory, and for an observer who waits as well as one who trades (Theorem~\ref{n:univ}). For exponential memory the order is attained and does not improve as the market approaches instability (Theorem~\ref{n:cost}, Proposition~\ref{n:nearcrit}). Two groups with half-lives between 5 and 9 days need more than 4{,}000 years of daily prices to be told apart (Table~\ref{n:tab_cal}).
-\item \emph{Composition is irrelevant for forecasting.} An observer who sees every group's weight and position forecasts the next price no better than one who sees prices alone and fits an autoregression, up to the error of estimating the kernel. That error does not depend on how close the groups are (Theorem~\ref{n:irr}). What is most expensive to learn from prices is therefore worth least for predicting them.
-\item \emph{Composition is needed for targeted action and under stress.} A market-wide limit needs only the kernel; halting one group needs composition (Proposition~\ref{n:target}). With position limits, calm prices carry no information about how close traders are to their limits, and stress reveals it (Theorem~\ref{n:sat_thm}). The exceptions to irrelevance that we identify are these channels, plus informed traders and exogenous flows of different persistence (Proposition~\ref{n:exc}).
-\end{enumerate}
-The three findings fit this account. Share estimates from prices are fragile because the data they need explode as the types' rules approach one another; that the market-impact coefficient cannot be separated from the traders' reaction coefficients \citep{lux2018} is the sufficiency of the kernel, in which only their product enters. Positions that respond to prices but do not lead them are what feedback trading predicts when positions are functions of past prices. And identifiers are the only route to targeted action at a cost that does not explode, with a precise value: tagging all but one memory class is enough, and the last tag is worth nothing (Theorem~\ref{n:partial}).
-
-\paragraph{Results.}
-\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
-\item \emph{Sufficiency and identification} (Theorems~\ref{n:suff} and~\ref{n:ident}). With a value, noise traders, an insider and a dealer, each following an arbitrary causal rule, two populations with the same kernel give the same prices, order flows, insider profits and dealer inventories, realization by realization, and so the same equilibria. Prices identify the trading weight at each memory rate and nothing finer; for any population, linear or not, they identify exactly its aggregate flow map.
-\item \emph{The cost of composition} (Theorems~\ref{n:cost} and~\ref{n:univ}, Proposition~\ref{n:nearcrit}). For any smooth family of rules, separating $K$ types within $\Delta$ needs data of order $\Delta^{-(4K-2)}$, passively or by probing. For exponential memory Prony's method attains this order and the barrier does not lift near instability: prices then reveal how close the market is to breaking, not who is trading.
-\item \emph{Which questions are cheap} (Theorem~\ref{n:questions}). A linear question about the population is answered at the usual statistical rate, whatever the number of groups and the spacing of their memories, exactly when its kernel has square-summable power-series coefficients; its exact cost is a problem of polynomial approximation.
-\item \emph{Forecast irrelevance} (Theorem~\ref{n:irr}, Proposition~\ref{n:exc}). Composition improves price forecasts by at most the cost of estimating the kernel, independently of the spacing; we list the channels through which it can matter.
-\item \emph{Supervision} (Section~\ref{n:sup}). Halting the largest group needs composition, at the cost above. Class-tagged flow removes the cost, and tagging all but one memory class is enough. Pooling markets in which the same algorithms trade in different proportions lowers the exponent without any tags. A group that can mimic another's memory caps what any price-based targeting can achieve.
-\item \emph{Surveillance over time} (Theorem~\ref{n:track}). When the population drifts, composition can be tracked only at the cube-root rate and not at all above a speed limit.
-\item \emph{Limits} (Section~\ref{n:sat}). With position limits the sufficient object becomes a measure over memory and leverage. Calm prices reveal only its linear part, the kernel; probes large enough to reach the limits identify the rest.
-\end{enumerate}
+\paragraph{The economic question.} A supervisor may know how strongly a market reacts to past prices while knowing little about which traders generate that response. These are different objects of inference. If every group's feedback is scaled by the same factor, the aggregate response is sufficient to evaluate the intervention in our model. If a particular group is halted, its contribution matters. This paper quantifies that distinction and the information supplied by class-tagged reports.
+
+\paragraph{The answer.} Under a common public signal and fixed causal rules, the population enters public market outcomes through its aggregate flow map. For linear rules this map is the feedback kernel. Equal kernels give equal public paths, insider profits and dealer inventories under the same shocks and strategies; the resulting equilibrium correspondence uses the same strategy spaces and public payoff maps. Individual feedback traders' payoffs need not agree. A counterfactual that changes their strategies requires a separate invariance or equilibrium argument.
+
+Three results organize the analysis. First, closely spaced types are weakly identified. A moment-matched pair of $K$-type populations has aggregate-flow differences of order $\Delta^{2K-1}$, leading to a Gaussian information lower bound of order $\Delta^{-(4K-2)}$. The smooth-family result requires common initial information, predictable probes and a derivative-moment bound uniform over the experiments being compared. It is a worst-case lower bound; some smooth families are never identified. Second, aggregate feedback can suffice for a decision even when the underlying population is hard to estimate. Proposition~\ref{n:target} gives the intervention comparison, and Corollary~\ref{n:decisionloss} expresses its consequence as expected decision loss. Third, reports that separately measure class flows change the observation experiment. In the exponential-memory model, tagging all but one memory class removes the spacing penalty for recovering class weights. An additional tag can still improve precision.
+
+\paragraph{A forecasting benchmark.} If the common pricing-error signal is observed, known parameters and zero initial group states make current positions reconstructible from its history. For an unknown fixed population in a stationary Gaussian market, a specified autoregressive predictor has excess forecast risk of order $\log T/T$ pointwise. This separates estimating an aggregate response from estimating its components. Theorem~\ref{n:uniformlog} adds a uniform cumulative log-loss bound under zero initialization and known Gaussian innovation variance. Neither result establishes a monotone decline in the value of positions or irrelevance for traded returns with hidden fundamentals. Theorem~\ref{n:dual} relates pairwise detection to forecast mismatch, while Theorem~\ref{n:shape} proves a recovery bound within separated shrinking clusters in a specified response experiment.
+
+\paragraph{Results and interpretation.} Theorems~\ref{n:suff}--\ref{n:ident} identify the sufficient public object; Theorems~\ref{n:cost}--\ref{n:univ} quantify identification costs in their specified experiments. Theorem~\ref{n:questions} classifies linear population functionals by their estimation cost. Section~\ref{n:sup} studies common interventions, targeting, partial disclosure and pooling across markets with shared memory rates. Section~\ref{n:dyn} considers bounded drift, and Section~\ref{n:sat} separates calm observations from observations that reach hard position limits. Illustrative calendar-year conversions depend on assumed noise, weight and spacing parameters; they are not estimates of a particular venue's identification horizon.
+
+The motivation is consistent with evidence of difficult parameter estimation in heterogeneous-agent models \citep{lux2018} and weak predictive content of large-trader positions in ten agricultural futures markets \citep{sanders2009}. Those findings neither identify the mechanism here nor test its intervention counterfactuals. The empirical exercises are reported with their registered outcomes and limitations.
 
 \paragraph{What is known.} Exponential memory is the oldest model of expectations: adaptive expectations \citep{cagan1956,nerlove1958}, geometric distributed lags \citep{koyck1954}, and \citet{muth1960}'s proof that exponentially weighted forecasts are optimal for a random walk observed with noise. The idea that an aggregate depends on a distribution of individual coefficients and not on the individuals is also old. \citet{granger1980} showed it for sums of autoregressions, \citet{forni1997} developed the general theory of aggregation and dynamics, \citet{zaffaroni2004} and \citet{pesaran2014} the large-economy and large-panel versions, and recovering the distribution is the disaggregation problem \citep{celov2008}. Results of the form ``two structures give the same data, so data cannot choose'' have a famous precedent in \citet{sargent1976}, whose observational equivalence of natural and unnatural rate theories settled what reduced-form data could say about monetary rules. A large literature models markets with heterogeneous fundamentalist and chartist traders \citep{brock1998,hommes2006} and estimates their shares from prices \citep{boswijk2007,franke2012,lux2018}; our results say which features of a population such estimates can recover from prices, and at what cost, when demand is linear in past prices. \citet{auclert2024} make the corresponding sufficient-statistic argument for household heterogeneity in macroeconomics. That a decision needs only a reduction of the data is the subject of sufficiency and the comparison of experiments \citep{blackwell1953,lecam1964}, and control theory distinguishes identifying a system from having data that are informative enough for a control objective \citep{vanwaarde2020}. The rate $(\Delta\eta)^{-(4K-2)}$ is the known minimax rate for mixtures with near-colliding components \citep{heinrich2018,wu2020}, and the uniqueness step behind the stress result is the uniqueness of networks with one hidden layer \citep{sussmann1992}. The exponent $2K-1$ in the gap between near-colliding populations is also the error amplification for super-resolution of $K$ near-colliding point sources \citep{batenkov2021}, of which Prony's method is the classical instance. That disaggregate information helps forecast an aggregate when the components have different dynamics is known from the literature on forecasting aggregates \citep{lutkepohl1987,hendry2011}; Proposition~\ref{n:exc}(b) is that result in this market. We do not claim these.
 
-\paragraph{What is new.} The market setting, the policy question, and six results with no counterpart in the literatures above. (a) Sufficiency holds path by path inside a strategic market, for every insider and dealer rule, so it transfers to every equilibrium concept, including when impact and persistence come from an inventory equilibrium (Supplement Theorem~\ref{thm:eqreduced}). (b) The memory rates are transmission zeros of the closed-loop price response, not poles, which is why passive data are no cheaper than probing (Supplement Theorem~\ref{thm:minphase}). (c) The barrier does not lift near instability, where one expects the loop to amplify differences between populations (Proposition~\ref{n:nearcrit}). (d) With partial disclosure the cost is set by the number of untagged memory classes, not traders, and the last tag is worth nothing (Theorem~\ref{n:partial}). (e) Without disclosure, the fact that an algorithm's memory is the same in every market it trades identifies it across markets (Theorem~\ref{n:pool}). (f) Every linear question about the population has an exact cost given by a problem of polynomial approximation, which sorts supervisory questions into cheap, boundary and expensive ones (Theorem~\ref{n:questions}). With position limits, calm prices carry exactly no information about how close traders are to their limits. Two results are new in this version. The cost of composition is not a feature of exponential memory: its lower bound holds for every smooth family of trading rules inside the feedback loop, with one proof for passive and probing observers (Theorem~\ref{n:univ}). And composition, which is so expensive to learn, is worth almost nothing for forecasting prices, by an amount that does not depend on how close the groups are (Theorem~\ref{n:irr}): the barrier and the irrelevance are two faces of the same sufficiency.
+\paragraph{Trader identity in market microstructure.} Identity disclosure already has an established information role. \citet{linnainmaa2012} study investor information inferred from broker identity and order flow. \citet{frino2026} examine delayed identity disclosure and price informativeness with informed traders. Those mechanisms differ from the common-public-signal benchmark here, and show why a general irrelevance claim would be inappropriate. Our intervention comparison concerns estimating response coefficients and assigning restrictions, rather than establishing that identity can have value for price discovery.
+
+Recent spectral-estimation work also develops single- and multi-snapshot stability bounds, including \citet{huangliu2026}. Its stated Fourier-measurement and noise assumptions differ from the feedback-market experiments here. We cite its existence and scope rather than infer a new statistical priority claim from the market application.
+
+\paragraph{Contribution relative to existing methods.} The statistical exponent and moment-matching method have close antecedents in finite-mixture estimation and super-resolution. Our contribution is their use in an endogenous feedback market with explicit observation contracts, combined with a comparison of common and class-specific interventions. Sufficiency is pathwise under common causal strategies; the price response identifies memory rates as transmission zeros under the mechanical-impact model. Partial disclosure changes the number of untagged classes, pooling uses shared rates with diverse market weights, and hard limits separate calm-path equivalence from stress identification. These are conditional market results. Local numerical rank checks do not establish global identification, and a lower bound alone does not provide an attainable rate for every smooth family.
 
