+\begin{proposition}[A global identification certificate for pooled moments]\label{prop:poolcertificate}
+Suppose $K$ is known and the normalized moments are $t_{d,m}=\sum_{i=1}^K W_{di}s_i^m$, with distinct ordered real nodes and strictly positive weights, observed for $d=1,\ldots,D$ and $m=0,\ldots,L$, $L\ge K$. Form the $D(L-K+1)\times K$ block-Hankel matrix and vector
+\[
+H_{(d,j),\ell}=t_{d,j+\ell},\qquad b_{(d,j)}=-t_{d,j+K},
+\quad 0\le j\le L-K,\quad0\le\ell<K.
+\]
+If $\operatorname{rank}H=K$, the moments identify all nodes and weights globally, up to relabelling, among populations with $K$ distinct nodes. At $L=l^*=K-1+\lceil K/D\rceil$, this condition holds outside a proper algebraic subset of the parameter space. On any compact ordered class with separated nodes, bounded weights, and $\operatorname{rank}H=K$ throughout, the moment map has a Lipschitz inverse and injective differential. On a class with nonempty interior, the global observation-cost exponent in Theorem~\ref{thm:pool} is therefore $2l^*$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Let $P(z)=\prod_i(z-s_i)=z^K+\sum_{\ell=0}^{K-1}c_\ell z^\ell$. Multiplying $P(s_i)=0$ by $W_{di}s_i^j$ and summing gives $Hc=b$. Full column rank makes $c$ unique. Any other $K$-node population with the same moments has a monic annihilating polynomial satisfying the same system, so it has the same polynomial and the same roots. The first $K$ moments in each market then determine its weights through the nonsingular Vandermonde matrix. This is a uniqueness argument, without an optimization search.
+
+For genericity let $q=\lceil K/D\rceil$ and $L=K-1+q$. With $V_{i,\ell}=s_i^\ell$, one has $H=BV$, where $B_{(d,j),i}=W_{di}s_i^j$, $0\le j<q$. Assign each node to one of $D$ groups of size at most $q$, give it weight one in its assigned market and zero elsewhere, and take distinct nodes. The columns of $B$ then form disjoint Vandermonde blocks, each of full column rank. Thus $H$ has rank $K$. A sufficiently small positive perturbation of every zero weight preserves a nonzero minor and gives strictly positive weights. The relevant minors are polynomials, so rank failure is a proper algebraic condition. This proves genericity; it does not assert full rank at every positive configuration.
+
+For stability of the inverse, $c=(H^\top H)^{-1}H^\top b$ is smooth wherever $H$ has full column rank. Roots of $P$ are locally smooth functions of its coefficients when they are simple, and each market's weights are smooth functions of the ordered roots and its first $K$ moments. These maps give a local smooth left inverse of the moment map, hence an injective differential. On the stated compact class, the smallest singular value of $H$ and the node gaps are bounded away from zero. Local inverse bounds, compactness and injectivity give a global Lipschitz inverse: otherwise pairs with a vanishing image-to-parameter distance ratio would either have distinct limits, contradicting injectivity, or a common limit, contradicting a local inverse bound. The upper rate follows by the rescaled-moment argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:pool}(ii); its lower rate holds on the class's interior by part (i).
+\end{proof}
+
+This certificate is the multi-channel annihilating-polynomial method underlying Prony reconstruction, not a new signal-processing principle. It supplies a direct condition for the financial pooling model and closes the generic global-uniqueness question at $l^*$. Rank-deficient exceptional classes, unknown numbers of types, misspecified shared memories, cross-impact, and a robust numerical rank decision under noise require further treatment. The smallest singular value matters: full rank in exact arithmetic alone is not a guarantee of useful finite-sample precision. The proof is on paper; exact rational tests exercise the recurrence and its reconstruction for $2\le K\le8$ and $1\le D\le K$.
+
+
+\paragraph{What this means.} Under the pooling assumption, an algorithm has a common memory across markets. Variation in its participation can then improve identification. Pooling price records from markets where the same algorithms trade in different proportions lowers the exponent of the composition barrier, from $4K-2$ to $2(K-1+\lceil K/D\rceil)$, without any disclosure. Cross-market surveillance is a partial substitute for tagging. The converse is a constraint on hiding: to stay as hidden as in a single market, the algorithms must trade in the same proportions in every market, so that the participation matrix has rank one. For two algorithms with half-lives between 5 and 9 days (memory rates $0.9\pm0.025$, total weight $0.06$, impact $0.5$, feedback budget $0.9$, passive Gaussian records), the Gauss and Gauss--Radau pair needs about $7{,}000$ years of daily prices in one market. In two markets in which the algorithms' shares differ by $50$ percentage points, the hardest pair found by a numerical search needs about $90$ years; with shares differing by $20$ points, about $4{,}000$. At half the memory spacing the one-market figure rises to $400{,}000$ years and the two-market figures to about $1{,}300$ and $55{,}000$. The gain grows like $(\Delta\eta)^{-2}$ as memories approach each other, and it depends on how differently the algorithms allocate across markets. These two-market figures come from a numerical search for the hardest pair under stated constraints; the true worst case can only be harder.
+
+\paragraph{Relation to the literature, and checks.} The mathematics is the multi-snapshot version of super-resolution, where several observations of the same nodes with different amplitudes are known to improve resolution \citep{li2022}; the contribution here is the explicit market observation contract, its local and rank-certified global cost, and its intervention interpretation. We do not claim the underlying multi-channel Prony method as new. The exponent is confirmed by the smallest singular value of the Jacobian in 60-digit arithmetic (local slopes $3,2$; $5,4,3$; $7,5,5,4$ for $K=2,3,4$) and by exact rank computations (\nolinkurl{research_extension/cross_market/}, which also holds the calibration scripts). The counting steps are machine-checked (\texttt{p1\_pool\_rows\_lt}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_rows\_ge}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_single\_market}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_many\_markets} in \nolinkurl{Paper1PoolingMarkets.lean}). Proposition~\ref{prop:poolcertificate} supplies a block-Hankel rank certificate for global recovery at $l^*$ and proves that it holds generically. A compact class entirely satisfying that certificate has $l_c=l_g=l^*$. Numerical multistart searches are supplementary diagnostics: approximate roots near colliding nodes do not certify distinct exact populations, and absence of a found alternative is not a proof. The fresh extended-search output and its residual checks are included in the revision audit. Monte Carlo runs of the local estimator of part~(ii), with the search started at the true configuration, confirm the attained rates: the weight error grows like $(\Delta\eta)^{-l^*}$ with fitted slopes $-3.02$, $-1.98$, $-4.92$ and $-2.95$ for $(K,D)=(2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,3)$, against $-3,-2,-5,-3$ (\nolinkurl{research_extension/theorem_checks/mc_rates.py}). The lower bound on joint records of all markets is machine-checked for any matched pair (\texttt{p1\_pool\_lower\_bound\_matched}, \nolinkurl{Paper1PoolingLower.lean}), with an explicit pair for two algorithms in two markets (\texttt{p1\_pool\_explicit\_pair\_K2D2}). The upper bounds are machine-checked under condition (R): the local minimum-distance estimator, and on a compact class on which the moment map is injective the global one, is within $(2/c)\,2^l e(\Delta\eta)^{-l}$ of the truth when every kernel tap up to order $l$ is within $e$; at order $2K-1$ the moment map is injective on ordered configurations with nonzero weights in one market, with injective derivative, so $l_g\le2K-1$ (\texttt{p1\_pool\_upper\_local}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_upper\_global}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_injective\_top}, \texttt{p1\_pool\_fderiv\_injective\_top}, \nolinkurl{Paper1PoolingUpper.lean}). Not covered: markets with cross-impact, and algorithms whose memory differs across markets.
 
